From 3a745fed6954a9d45e93285d205d752495b27a9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:36:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] [clangd] A check that found a module's inputs as they
 were holds for a while

A memoized dependency scan and a BMI's validation verdict are reused after
every input they read is checked again, and a project's own inputs are
checked one by one for every request: a file importing modules whose global
module fragments include a large header set re-stats them all on every
completion, nearly a second on Windows for a few dozen modules.

With --module-input-recheck=<seconds>, a check that found the inputs of a
scan or a verdict as they were holds for that long while the client reports
no file change: a saved document, or a watched file, makes the next check
read them all again (an edit too, with --use-dirty-headers, where modules
are built from drafts). A change the client does not see is found at the
next check after the interval, as clang's own once-per-build-session module
validation finds one at the next session. The BMI itself is checked every
time. The default, 0, checks every time, as before.

A slow completion's cost line counts the checks that still held.
---
